const realLifeExamples={
 'RE-01':['An estate agency receives the same buyer inquiry from a website form and a second contact channel. Two agents could follow up independently and give the buyer conflicting information.','The coordinator reviews the buyer details and the existing contact match instead of treating both submissions as unrelated leads.','The source demonstrates lead scoring and duplicate-match handling; the separate CRM handoff normalizes contact identity.','The team gets a prioritized inquiry and contact preview to review. The local demo does not send the buyer a drip campaign.'],
 'RE-02':['A transaction coordinator is handling several property purchases. A change to one transaction date can make a manually maintained deadline list unreliable.','The coordinator supplies the relevant transaction dates to the calculation rather than copying dates between spreadsheets.','The source applies its milestone rules and calculates the resulting deadlines. The reviewer checks those rules against the transaction requirements.','The coordinator receives a deadline list to verify before advancing work. The calculation is not legal approval or a completed compliance review.'],
 'RE-03':['A qualification team has more inquiries than it can review immediately. A promising inquiry can be buried among incomplete or unsuitable submissions.','The reviewer compares lead attributes using the same scoring rules rather than relying only on arrival order.','The source calculates comparative scores and routing decisions from example attributes. Inspect the rule contributions before trusting the ordering.','The reviewer sees which inquiries deserve attention and why. Assignment or outreach still requires the appropriate operational review.'],
 'RE-04':['A commercial property analyst is preparing a potential acquisition for a committee. A proposed property needs comparison with relevant properties before anyone treats the price as reasonable.','The analyst examines the property record and the comparison records used by the demonstration.','The source produces comparable-property analysis. The analyst must inspect the inputs and assumptions rather than treating an aggregate as an independent valuation.','The analysis supports a committee discussion. It does not approve an investment or establish that the sample records reflect the current market.'],
 'REC-01':['A recruiter finds a candidate whose experience appears relevant, but the candidate has not granted the required consent for the next recruiting step.','The team separates a possible skills match from permission to use or advance the candidate record.','The source checks matching conditions and consent gates. A strong match does not override the consent requirement.','Only eligible matches move to recruiter review. The local example does not autonomously contact candidates or make a hiring decision.'],
 'MKT-01':['An agency analyst notices unusual spending across advertising accounts. Acting on the first apparent anomaly could change a campaign before anyone understands the cause.','The analyst reviews the normalized advertising-control inputs and the suggested budget action.','The source evaluates its control rules and produces a recommendation with an approval boundary.','The account owner can investigate and decide whether a change is appropriate. The local demo does not change a live ad account or prove improved returns.'],
 'SAAS-01':['A software company has many trial accounts, but its team cannot personally review all of them. Product usage can help identify accounts worth examining.','A customer-success representative reviews the bundled usage signals instead of assuming every signup has the same needs.','The source turns usage examples into account scores that support a review decision.','The team receives a signal for follow-up planning. A usage score is not proof that an account will convert, and the demo establishes no conversion uplift.'],
 'SAAS-02':['A subscription renewal fails. Without a consistent recovery process, a customer might receive duplicate reminders or be moved to the wrong billing state.','The billing operator checks the current failed-payment state before selecting the next recovery step.','The source demonstrates the allowed dunning state transitions using sample recovery states.','The operator can inspect what should happen next. The local run does not charge the customer, send a payment reminder or establish provider retry behavior.'],
 'SAAS-03':['A customer-success manager needs to understand which accounts may need attention before renewal. A risk score alone does not explain the right intervention.','The analyst examines the synthetic customer inputs, model output and available explanation context together.','The source demonstrates risk output and intervention context, including the explanation fallback where applicable.','The manager reviews the signal before deciding whether and how to contact an account. The example does not establish real-world predictive accuracy or prevented churn.'],
 'SAAS-04':['A revenue analyst sees a mismatch between usage records and billing records near month-end. Closing the cycle without investigation could leave an unresolved charge discrepancy.','The analyst compares the sample usage and billing records through one reconciliation process.','The source returns reconciliation results and exceptions, making the mismatch visible for investigation.','The analyst investigates the exceptions before closing. The local example does not modify invoices or certify revenue recognition.'],
 'ECOM-01':['A merchant sells a 500 ml insulated bottle. While preparing content for another market, a draft changes the capacity to 750 ml. Publishing it could promise a product the merchant does not sell.','The catalog reviewer compares the draft with the authoritative product facts. In this illustrative story, capacity is a protected fact.','The governance logic checks protected facts before accepting a candidate change. A factual mismatch is rejected with a reason rather than being treated as harmless translation.','The reviewer receives the rejected change and can request a corrected draft. The local demo demonstrates the protection rule; the bottle and quantities illustrate the use case and are not claims about its exact fixture. No product is published.'],
 'ECOM-02':['A shopper leaves a review describing a potentially unsafe product experience. Treating it as an ordinary negative review could put a routine public reply ahead of a necessary investigation.','The review team distinguishes the complaint type before approving a response.','The source demonstrates complaint classification and response-review routing, with safety complaints requiring closer inspection.','A reviewer examines the complaint and response queue before approving a public reply. The local example does not post a response or resolve a safety investigation.'],
 'ECOM-03':['An online store receives an inventory observation that is older than the stock information already under review. Accepting it blindly could replace newer evidence with stale stock data.','The operations team checks freshness and conflicting observations before considering an update.','The source demonstrates reconciliation and freshness decisions using example inventory records.','The team receives the decision and can investigate stale or conflicting evidence. The local run does not establish a live warehouse or Shopify stock update.'],
 'ECOM-04':['A customer sends a support request containing information that should not be repeated across every team. The request also needs an appropriate response priority.','The support desk prepares a reduced record for the next queue and checks the applicable service-level routing.','The source demonstrates redaction and SLA routing from sample support requests.','The receiving reviewer inspects the destination and the redacted content before handling the case. A local routing result is not proof that an external support system accepted it.'],
 'ACC-01':['An accounts-payable team receives an invoice that may already have been processed, along with a request to use different bank details. Paying immediately could duplicate a payment or use an unverified destination.','The operator checks invoice matching, duplicate signals and banking exceptions before asking for payment approval.','The source demonstrates the matching and exception controls. A banking change or duplicate concern must not be hidden by a normal invoice total.','The operator receives a blocked or exception result to investigate. The local demo does not authorize payment, change bank details or transfer money.'],
 'CS-01':['A support lead reviews an answer that sounds confident but may not be supported by the approved guidance. Reusing it could spread the same weakness to future responses.','The quality reviewer checks the answer and the associated knowledge-feedback record while minimizing unnecessary personal information.','The source demonstrates answer-quality checks, redaction and escalation output.','The lead reviews weak support and knowledge gaps before changing guidance. This is evidence for a quality discussion, not proof that every generated answer is grounded.'],
 'IMP-01':['An import-company operator is preparing a task that may involve several operational responsibilities. A proposed action needs traceable evidence and a clear decision boundary.','The operator follows the synthetic task through the public TypeScript agent-system prototype.','The prototype provides task output that can be inspected alongside its source and review architecture.','The operator reviews the evidence before any material action. This public example is separate from the full private PORT / OS operating app and does not prove autonomous execution of an import operation.'],
 'PM-01':['A tenant reports a possible gas leak while a coordinator is also processing routine maintenance requests. Treating it like a normal repair would put urgency behind scheduling convenience.','The coordinator distinguishes the emergency from routine work and checks access approval for non-emergency requests.','The gas-leak fixture routes to human emergency review. Routine work uses access and vendor-review rules; dispatch remains disabled in the demonstration.','A human operator receives the priority and reasons. The local program does not dispatch a contractor or replace the emergency response process.'],
 'EA-01':['An executive assistant sees the same commitment in two updates, while another update changes its owner or meaning under the same identifier. A naive import could create duplicate or contradictory work.','The assistant keeps the source and commitment identity visible and checks authorization and owner confirmation.','Identical identifiers collapse; conflicts are rejected and unauthorized items are excluded. Unconfirmed commitments remain in owner review.','The assistant gets a reviewable commitment register without automatic external follow-up. The local extension persists decisions in SQLite; source permission fields remain trusted fixture inputs.'],
 'CON-01':['A contractor is ready to proceed with a change request, but the available certificate has expired or the approvals refer to an earlier request version.','The coordinator compares certificate dates, the current version and the identities of the approving people.','The source routes missing independent current approvals or expired certificates to exception review and records a linked approval ledger.','The coordinator gets reasons to resolve before progressing. The demo keeps release authority false; it does not authorize work or payment.'],
 'MED-01':['A referral coordinator receives a packet missing one of the documents required by the demonstration checklist. Sending it forward without review would leave staff chasing the same missing information later.','The coordinator checks storage consent, the fictional payer identifier and the supplied document names.','The source lists missing documents and routes unknown payers or missing consent to staff review.','Staff receive a completeness queue to work through. The payer checklist is fictional, and this example makes no clinical or real payer authorization decision.'],
 'LEGAL-01':['A new matter inquiry names a party that resembles an entity on an adverse-party list. Automatically treating a near match as either clear or confirmed could misdirect intake.','The intake coordinator compares party names and adverse-entity names while keeping ambiguous identities visible.','Exact matches require conflict review; ambiguous names require identity review. Even a no-match result still requires attorney clearance.','The reviewer gets the reason and appropriate queue. The demo does not clear a conflict or create a legal matter.'],
 'INS-01':['A claims intake packet contains a possible duplicate and dates that need checking against the provided policy period. A complete-looking form can still contain unresolved exceptions.','The coordinator reviews the loss date, policy dates, document list and duplicate signal together.','The source identifies date exceptions and missing evidence, then routes the packet for adjuster review.','The adjuster gets the reasons and missing-document list. The local example does not decide coverage or pay a claim.'],
 'INV-01':['A wholesale desk needs to decide whether a stock position deserves replenishment attention. An order suggestion based on unchecked quantities could be misleading.','The operator reviews the bundled stock and replenishment inputs using the same quantity rules each time.','The local source calculates its reorder recommendation and validates the supplied quantities.','The buyer inspects the recommendation and its inputs before any purchasing action. This is a fictional wholesale demonstration, not a sent purchase order.'],
 'CRM-01':['An agency account moves between teams. Without a clear owner and version, a second handoff can overwrite a more recent decision or leave both teams thinking the other owns it.','The account coordinator checks canonical account identity, ownership and the handoff version.','The source applies the agency handoff rules and makes conflicting ownership or version conditions reviewable.','The team receives a handoff result to inspect before a provider-specific CRM update. The fictional agency example does not prove a deployed CRM workflow.'],
 'AGT-01':['A support assistant is asked a question whose answer may belong to a different tenant or may not exist in the approved material. A plausible answer would be harmful if it used the wrong evidence.','The operator reviews the tenant and permission context together with the available approved source material.','The local extension uses tenant-scoped lexical retrieval and exact-source extractive answers. Missing or tied evidence leads to abstention; altered quotes or fabricated citations fail validation.','The reviewer sees the source evidence or the abstention reason. This is an offline authored evaluation, not a live language-model answer or a general prompt-injection benchmark.'],
 'OPS-01':['A booking handoff has an uncertain outcome after an attempted delivery. Retrying immediately could create a duplicate if the first attempt actually succeeded.','The operator examines the handoff state and recovery decision before deciding whether another attempt is appropriate.','The source demonstrates booking handoff recovery with explicit uncertainty handling in its bounded scenarios.','The operator receives a recovery result to inspect and reconcile. The fictional booking example does not establish a live appointment booking or production retry guarantee.']
};
function makeSlides(){
    const deck=leanSlides(),p=selected,profile=profiles[p.id],e=record?.evidence;
    const [audience,input,output,next]=p.definition||projectDefinition(p.id);
    const [focus,decision,proof,metric]=talkingPoints[p.id];
    const para=(label,text)=>`<h3>${escape(label)}</h3><p>${escape(text)}</p>`;
    const graph=profile?`<div class="deck-visible-graph">${workflowSVG(profile.architecture,{prefix:'presentation-context'}).svg}</div><p class="meta">Actual local wrapper structure; the GHL endpoint is simulated.</p>`:'<p>Workflow details are loading. Reopen the presentation after loading completes.</p>';
    const outputPreview=e?`<pre>${escape((e.businessEvidence?.output||e.error||'No business output retained.').slice(0,1800))}</pre><p class="meta">Saved output excerpt; full output is available in the main explanation.</p>`:para('Expected output type',output)+para('Evidence status','No run is selected. This describes the design, not an observed result.');
    const support=[
      para('At a glance',p.demo)+`<dl><dt>Receives</dt><dd>${escape(input)}</dd><dt>Produces</dt><dd>${escape(output)}</dd><dt>Next step</dt><dd>${escape(next)}</dd></dl>`,
      `<ol class="deck-scenario"><li>${para('The starting point',input)}</li><li>${para('The rule we apply',decision)}</li><li>${para('What the reviewer gets',output)}</li></ol><p class="meta">Illustrative walkthrough of the bundled scenario, not a named client outcome.</p>`,
      para('Why the transformation matters',focus)+para('What to inspect',proof)+para('What remains a human decision',next)+para('CRM data boundary','The business result and the sample contact are separate. This demo does not copy the raw business record into GHL.'),
      graph+para('How to read the diagram','Follow the connections from the trigger through the business step and contact checks. Node names come from the runnable workflow JSON.'),
      profile?`<table><thead><tr><th>Project tool</th><th>Why it is here</th></tr></thead><tbody>${profile.tools.map(t=>`<tr><td>${escape(t.name)}</td><td>${escape(t.role)}<small>${escape(t.status)}</small></td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table>`:para('Project source',p.source),
      para('Inspect this evidence',proof)+para('Handling an exception','Preserve the failed result, locate the failed step and check whether a write occurred before retrying.')+para('Where review takes over',next)+para('A useful question','What input would change this decision, and which rule in the source explains why?'),
      '<section class="deck-source"><h3>Business source preview</h3><p>The actual local file will load here.</p></section>',
      profile?`<h3>Named contact checks</h3><ul>${profile.acceptance.cases.slice(0,6).map(c=>`<li><strong>${c.passed?'Pass':'Fail'}</strong> · ${escape(c.name)}</li>`).join('')}</ul><h3>Business regression examples</h3><ul>${profile.regression.cases.slice(0,4).map(c=>`<li><strong>${c.passed?'Pass':'Fail'}</strong> · ${escape(c.name)}</li>`).join('')}</ul><p class="meta">${escape(profile.regression.scope)} Full lists remain expandable.</p>`:para('Evidence pending','The project profile has not finished loading.'),
      outputPreview,
      `<h3>A short demonstration route</h3><ol><li>Show the sample input and explain its purpose.</li><li>Follow the decision through the source and workflow.</li><li>Inspect the result and one rejected case.</li></ol>${para('Business measure to evaluate',metric)}${para('Before production','Validate provider behavior and permissions, test the broader workflow, and agree on acceptance criteria with the intended users.')}`
    ];
    const example=realLifeExamples[p.id];
    if(example){
      deck[1]=['A concrete example',`<h2>A real-life situation this project addresses</h2>${para('The situation',example[0])}${para('How the person starts',example[1])}<p class="meta">Illustrative scenario based on the project’s scope, not a named client engagement or a claim of measured results.</p>`];
      support[1]=para('What the system checks',example[2])+para('What the person receives and does next',example[3]);
      support[2]=para('In this scenario',example[0])+para('How the result is used',example[3]);
    }
    return deck.map(([label,body],i)=>[label,`<div class="deck-spread"><article class="deck-explanation">${body}</article><section class="deck-support" aria-label="Supporting detail">${support[i]}</section></div>`]);
}
function updateDeckTools(){
    let agenda=$('deckAgenda');if(!agenda){agenda=document.createElement('select');agenda.id='deckAgenda';agenda.setAttribute('aria-label','Presentation slide');$('deck').querySelector('.deck-controls').prepend(agenda);agenda.onchange=()=>{slideIndex=Number(agenda.value);showSlide()};}
    agenda.innerHTML=slides.map((s,i)=>`<option value="${i}">${i+1} · ${escape(s[0])}</option>`).join('');agenda.value=String(slideIndex);
    const slide=$('slide');slide.scrollTop=0;
    for(const button of slide.querySelectorAll('[data-deck-source]'))button.onclick=async()=>{
      const artifact=profiles[selected.id]?.artifacts.find(a=>a.key===button.dataset.deckSource);if(!artifact)return;
      let panel=slide.querySelector('.deck-source');if(!panel){panel=document.createElement('section');panel.className='deck-source';slide.append(panel)}
      panel.textContent='Reading local source…';
      try{const response=await fetch(artifact.url);if(!response.ok)throw Error('Source request failed ('+response.status+')');const text=await response.text();if(!panel.isConnected)return;
        panel.innerHTML=`<h3>${escape(artifact.label)}</h3><label>Find in source <input type="search" aria-label="Find in source"></label><p role="status"></p><pre tabindex="0">${escape(text.split('\n').map((line,i)=>String(i+1).padStart(4)+'  '+line).join('\n'))}</pre>`;
        panel.querySelector('input').oninput=e=>{const query=e.target.value.toLowerCase(),lines=text.split('\n'),line=lines.findIndex(l=>l.toLowerCase().includes(query));panel.querySelector('[role=status]').textContent=query?(line<0?'No match':'First match: line '+(line+1)):'';panel.querySelector('pre').scrollTop=query&&line>=0?Math.max(0,(line-3)*20.8):0;};
      }catch(error){panel.textContent=error.message;}
    };
    if(slideIndex===6)slide.querySelector('[data-deck-source="source"]')?.click();
}



function presentationRouteHTML(){return `<section class="presentation-route" aria-label="Recommended presentation route"><div><span class="eyebrow">A focused introduction</span><h2>Three examples. Three engineering decisions.</h2><p>Start here to understand the work before exploring the full collection.</p></div><div class="route-steps"><article><span>01 / Automation</span><h3>Protect a product fact</h3><p>ECOM-01: follow an unsafe content change through its rule, n8n wrapper and recorded local handoff.</p><button id="startAutomationRoute">Open ECOM-01 →</button></article><article><span>02 / Human review</span><h3>Correct a supplier record</h3><p>ShelfSense: compare 24 with the source value of 12, correct the draft and approve the reviewed fields.</p><a href="/engineering#ENG-05">Open ShelfSense →</a></article><article><span>03 / Coding</span><h3>Prove a repair</h3><p>Repair Studio: inspect the duplicate-event bug, the Codex-assisted candidate and actual before-and-after tests.</p><a href="/engineering#ENG-09">Open Repair Studio →</a></article></div></section>`;}
